  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 310 AM EDT MON SEP 21 2026\n\nThe extended period begins Tuesday evening with a strong ~583 dam \nhigh located across Northern Ontario and the Upper Great Lakes. \nThis high works to suppress an upper level low/trough south as it\nprogresses eastward into the Ohio Valley. Meanwhile, eastern \nKentucky sits on the cool, northern side of a stalled frontal \nboundary draped across Tennessee, North Carolina, and the Deep \nSouth. Between the stalled front to our south and multiple \nperturbations rotating around the approaching upper level low, the\narea should see rounds of showers and thunderstorms Tuesday \nevening and Wednesday. This could lead to isolated instances of \nflash flooding before the main trough axis finally shifts south \nand east on Thursday.\n\nFrom Thursday onward, the upper-level trough amplifies and becomes\na closed low off the East Coast. As the trough axis shifts east \nof the Commonwealth, cooler and much drier air arrives. Under \npersistent northerly flow and surface high pressure, seasonably \ncool weather takes hold: an extended period of daytime highs in \nthe 60s-70s and nighttime lows in the 40s-50s.",
